13
00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:38.270
to be able to move
quickly and get great feedback

14
00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:39.780
on everything that you're building.

15
00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:44.170
And this is a thing that's
well understood in more

16
00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:47.770
traditional forms of software, but
is shifting as many of

17
00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.660
us start to build LLM-powered applications.
. If

18
00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.220
we compare traditional
software to LLM-based applications,

19
00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:55.780
we can see a few
fundamental differences.

20
00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:00.220
Overall, from a behavior perspective,
traditional software tends

21
00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:03.060
to be focused around things that
are predefined.

22
00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:06.380
We know what the inputs are,
and we know as a

23
00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:08.800
result of any particular input
what the output will be.

24
00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:10.880
And that's
fairly straightforward to test for.

25
00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:13.940
By contrast,
in LLM based applications,

26
00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:18.800
we know what the inputs are,
but we get a set of possible outputs.

27
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.040
So it's
more probabilistic in nature.

28
00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:23.920
Many of these applications
are based on natural language.

29
00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:25.080
This could be highly subjective.

30
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.100
and if your application
does things like summarizing,

31
00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.120
then it's
quite possible that there will

32
00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:35.360
be many good outcomes. Outcomes
that are good enough and would

33
00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:38.660
be considered correct, there
are also many incorrect

34
00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:40.260
possible outcomes or outputs.

35
00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.980
And so the approach to
testing can be quite different.

36
00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:47.470
For example, if you were to prompt
an LLM to answer the question,

37
00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:49.580
is Italy a good place to take a
vacation?

38
00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:51.860
One answer could be yes.

39
00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.280
You can go to Rome, go to Florence,
you can go to Venice,

40
00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:58.300
you can also explore museums,
you could explore beaches.

41
00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:01.000
Another answer could be
a single word answer.

42
00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:01.680
Yes.

43
00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:06.000
Which one is considered better
will depend on your use case. It's

44
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.690
also important to note that LLMs
can produce harmful responses. They

45
00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:12.120
might be toxic, they
might be offensive.

46
00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:17.300
And so LLMs bring new challenges
to application testing

47
00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:19.180
compared to traditional software.

48
00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:23.830
To deal with these new testing
challenges, AI researchers developed

49
00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:28.640
the concept of evaluations or evals
to assess how LLMs are doing

50
00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:31.180
at specific tasks.
There are many common

51
00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:32.340
data sets for different tasks.

52
00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:37.780
Examples include MMLU, HeLaSwag,
and human eval. LLMs are often

53
00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:40.680
tested on different data sets so
researchers have a

54
00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:43.920
point of comparison between models.
However, what we're going to talk about

55
00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.960
is building your own
application and testing for

56
00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:49.260
your specific use case.

57
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.540
Okay, so as I mentioned, there's
some standard benchmarks, these

58
00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:55.200
are some examples, but
these are not necessarily going to

59
00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.160
