00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:39.590
keep checking the quality
of your work and

74
00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:42.260
keep checking the quality
of your application, again,

75
00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:44.920
across larger team,
across time, and that's what

76
00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:47.920
we're going to build to
over the course of

77
00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:51.910
this set of lessons so once we have
the ability to perform

78
00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:54.580
automated evals it's important
for us to understand

79
00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:57.240
what we're looking for
and when we should

80
00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:59.910
be performing these automated
evals in terms of

81
00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:02.910
what there's four main
areas that we think about

82
00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:05.570
first context adherence or
groundedness, which is the

83
00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:08.570
question of whether the
LLM response aligns with the

84
00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:09.900
provided context or guidelines.

85
00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.750
Next is context relevance, and that's
the question of whether

86
00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:16.300
the retrieved context is
relevant to the original

87
00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:20.440
query or prompt. Next, we
have correctness or accuracy, which

88
00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:23.220
is really, does the LLM output
align with the

89
00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:26.240
provided ground truth and the expected
results?

90
00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:29.980
How close is it to what we would
anticipate in the given scenario?

91
00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:33.720
And then finally, we're
concerned with bias and toxicity, which

92
00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:36.350
are really negative potential
that exist in the

93
00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:37.660
world of LLM-powered apps.

94
00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:41.980
First, bias should be favoritism
or prejudice towards or

95
00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:46.340
away from certain groups.
And then toxicity, harmful words or

96
00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:50.300
implicit wording that
is offensive to certain groups.

97
00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:54.790
And then in terms of when,
in a traditional software model, we're

98
00:04:54.790 --> 00:04:58.310
typically testing after every change, whether
that's bug fixes, feature updates,

99
00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:00.230
or even
data changes or changes

100
00:05:00.230 --> 00:05:03.120
to the model, which might
be a different case

101
00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:05.360
when you're evaluating from
an LOM perspective.

102
00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:10.000
If that becomes slow or takes
too long you can also start

103
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:12.420
to pull out
more comprehensive testing

104
00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:15.160
to specific points like pre-deployment.

105
00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:19.970
So doing more comprehensive testing
at the point that you're looking at pushing

106
00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:21.920
something into a
production environment or

107
00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:25.800
post deployment because some of these
changes might occur once your software

108
00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:28.390
is actually executing in a production
environment, which

109
00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:32.280
is again a bit of a shift from
our traditional software models.

